 ## Morphology and anatomy
 
-The corm lacks conducting cells; the strand begins at the base of the aerial axis and grades downward into files of dark cells without thickenings (Kerp 2017). The aerial strand is wide, wholly thick-walled, and has narrow annular to helical thickenings at the base. Outer cortex is three to five cells thick with large intercellular spaces, larger near stomata; the inner cortex has elongate cells (150 to 170 × 30 to 50 µm); a single hypodermis and scattered glands also occur (Kerp 2017).
+The corm lacks conducting cells; the strand begins at the base of the aerial axis and grades downward into files of dark cells without thickenings (Kerp 2017). The aerial strand is wide and wholly thick-walled. Under white light it shows narrow annular to helical thickenings at the base (Kerp 2017), long read as tracheids. Kenrick and Long (2026) re-imaged it by confocal microscopy with 3D modelling. They find wall ingrowths like those of transfer cells, which grow from discrete papillate loci and form threads across the cell lumen, unlike the secondary thickenings of tracheids, and cell form that varies along the plant. On their reading the strand is a single conducting tissue, probably for solute transport as well as water, and not xylem; Cascales-Miñana et al. (2019) had reaffirmed the tracheid reading. The dispute is open. Outer cortex is three to five cells thick with large intercellular spaces, larger near stomata; the inner cortex has elongate cells (150 to 170 × 30 to 50 µm); a single hypodermis and scattered glands also occur (Kerp 2017).
 
 Eggert (1974) established that the sporangia are branched into two to four columellate lobes sharing one sporogenous cavity, with no unbranched sporangia found. In Kerp's account each lobe's strand forks below the base but does not enter; the columella fills half to two-thirds of the length; the cavity is inverted-cup shaped; the sporangium probably opened through an apical pore. Spores develop mostly in tetrahedral tetrads but dyads, ring tetrads and aborted tetrads are frequent, indicating irregular meiosis (Eggert 1974; Kerp 2017).
 
